WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.720
In the film industry, a single film can make or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.760
break a career. This has been the&nbsp;
case since the dawn of Hollywood.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.600
The 2008 vampire romance movie&nbsp;
"Twilight" set its leads up for stardom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.040
based on a best-selling book series&nbsp;
with a built-in, eager fan base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.120
"The Twilight Saga" resulted in five films&nbsp;
and was a pop culture phenomenon at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.400
but not every star of the franchise&nbsp;
reaped the long-term rewards.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.920
While two of the leads, Kristen Stewart and&nbsp;
Robert Pattinson, have been able to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.440
critical and commercial success following&nbsp;
the end of the "Twilight" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.720
the series' third lead, Taylor&nbsp;
Lautner, has not been as fortunate.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.400
This is actually kind of a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.200
seeing as the actor's role as the&nbsp;
perennially shirtless werewolf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.600
Jacob Black, looked like a pretty safe pass to&nbsp;
stardom, and Lautner was perfectly convincing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.160
in the role — as convincing as anyone could be&nbsp;
playing a smoldering, young, jacked wolfman.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.200
"So you're a werewolf."

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.160
"Yeah. Last time I checked."

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.360
Nevertheless, Lautner, who played&nbsp;
Jacob across all five films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.520
has failed to turn his "Twilight" exposure to&nbsp;
A-list stardom, while Pattinson and Stewart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.680
have both managed to star in a healthy mix&nbsp;
of mainstream hits and critical darlings.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.640
In fact, since the release of "The Twilight&nbsp;
Saga: Breaking Dawn – Part 2" in 2012, Lautner's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.720
appearances in movies or television has been&nbsp;
noticeably more limited than you might assume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.600
However, this relatively unimpressive trajectory&nbsp;
hasn't necessarily been Lautner's own fault.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.880
In fact, one might argue that his&nbsp;
dwindling career can be traced back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.920
to a single film — an action thriller that&nbsp;
single-handedly ruined Taylor Lautner's career.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.120
"That doesn't look like me."

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.600
To be perfectly honest, Lautner is no stranger&nbsp;
to movies that got a poor critical reception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.200
The "Twilight" movies weren't beloved by&nbsp;
critics, and his first starring vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.760
"The Adventures of Sharkboy and Lavagirl,"&nbsp;
was disliked by both critics and audiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.080
The 2010 ensemble comedy "Valentine's&nbsp;
Day," which to be fair he only appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.280
in relatively briefly, has a&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes score of 18%.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.240
"And Felicia, what is it&nbsp;
about Willy that's so cool?"

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.200
"Besides the fact that he's totally hot?"

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.840
However, if you're looking to&nbsp;
pinpoint a particular film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.080
took a heavy toll on Lautner's career&nbsp;
prospects, his 2011 starring vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.800
"Abduction," ended up with a Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;score in the dreaded single digits: a measly 5%.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.400
The film's box office results&nbsp;
weren't very encouraging either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.200
as the film grossed just over $28 million&nbsp;
domestically on a $35 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.400
relying on its international box&nbsp;
office totals to turn a profit.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.160
This might not have reflected too well&nbsp;
on Lautner as a bankable leading man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.640
seeing as "Abduction" was the first starring role&nbsp;
he did after the initial success of "Twilight."

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.880
This was his opportunity to show Hollywood he had&nbsp;
what it took to be an action hero and a dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.880
lead. "Abduction" not connecting with audiences&nbsp;
or critics seemed to signal that Lautner would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.760
be unable to carry the franchise's success on&nbsp;
to other projects, and his acting CV since then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.680
makes it fairly clear that productions have&nbsp;
been somewhat wary of casting him in leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.880
roles ever since. Lautner's last movie role&nbsp;
to date came in the 2016 film "Run The Tide."

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.720
"Are you serious?"

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.200
Though "Abduction" certainly didn't do any&nbsp;
favors for Taylor Lautner's action chops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.480
it doesn't necessarily mean that&nbsp;
his days as an actor are over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.800
Just look at his "The Twilight Saga" co-star&nbsp;Robert Pattinson, who spent many post-"Twilight"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.120
years gravitating toward relatively obscure&nbsp;
and creepy dramas and arthouse films, and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.600
only recently started returning to comparatively&nbsp;mainstream fare, like Christopher Nolan's "Tenet,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.794
and Matt Reeves' upcoming "The Batman."

00:03:09.794 --> 00:03:16.640
While Pattinson went artsy, Lautner has been quietly reinventing himself as a bit of&nbsp;a comedian. He played Dr. Cassidy Cascade in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.720
the second season of "Scream Queens," a series&nbsp;with plenty of funny moments, and he replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.600
none other than Andy Samberg as the central&nbsp;character of the TV show "Cuckoo" after Season 1.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.280
"’Cuz I can do this!"

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.480
"You can do what?"

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.080
"This! Right here! Maybe I'm&nbsp;
already doing it! I don't know."

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.200
In 2015, the "Twilight" star&nbsp;
had a prominent role in Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.600
Sandler's comedy western, "The Ridiculous 6."

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.120
"Gol' dang!"

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.520
According to IMDb, Lautner is currently filming&nbsp;"Home Team," which is produced by Sandler's Happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.560
Madison Productions. Like "The Ridiculous 6," the&nbsp;movie is scheduled to debut directly on Netflix.&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.880
Apart from the occasional comedic role,&nbsp;
Lautner has recently kept himself busy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.760
real estate deals, as he bought a luxurious&nbsp;
mansion in Agoura Hills, California in 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.760
So even though he might not be the first person&nbsp;
Hollywood calls for action movies these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.280
Taylor Lautner seems to be living his&nbsp;
best life, and keeps himself occupied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.960
with fun projects that have very little&nbsp;
to do with his days as a hunky werewolf.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.200
And if you're curious about revisiting those days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.640
the five "Twilight" films are currently&nbsp;
all available to view on Netflix.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:16.420
"Thank you."

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:18.320
"No problem."

